<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Senkaku Islands Clash, 2019</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Senkaku/Diaoyu Island Conflict, 2019&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Created Under CMANO Version 1.14.5 - Build 998.12 &lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;–&lt;/FONT&gt; Database 475&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Rebuilt to CMO with "deep rebuild" 27 November 2019&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Al "Beirutdude" Sandrik&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;The Time Is: 29 November 2019 14:00 Zulu/22:00 Tokyo Time&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Senkaku Islands/Diaoyu Islands/East China Sea Sea&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Playable as the Japanese/U.S. Side&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;Thanks to Whicker, Excroat3, Aceofspadeszzzzzz, Maji and LMychajluk for their playtest help&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;2 Days Duration&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Background: The Senkaku/Diaoyu Islands are disputed by the Peoples Republic of China (PRC), Taiwan and Japan, with Japan currently controlling the islands. For decades they were little more than a navigational markers and a fishing grounds until in in the late 60s when oil reserves were discovered near the islands. This increased in 1971 as the United States turned over control of the Ryukyu Chain, including the Senkaku, to Japan. Jockeying for control of the islands really began in earnest in the 1990s with the situation becoming more nationalistic during the 2010s. By 2016 the dispute had reached a fever pitch when the United States confirmed it was committed to assisting the Japanese Self Defense Forces with the defense of the Islands. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Time line: &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Summer 2018; the United States (U.S.) and the Peoples Republic of China (PRC) trade tit for tat tariff increases on each other increasing tensions between the two largest economies on the planet.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;September 2018; as tensions increase, bilateral military ties and confidence building meetings are postponed or outright canceled. The USS Wasp is denied a port visit to Hong Kong during the third week of the month. Later on September 30th the USS Decatur and a PRC Type-52 Destroyer came within 45 feet of collision when the PLAN vessel cut across her bow during a "Freedom of Navigation" operation near Johnson Reef. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;October 2018; A PLA Navy (PLAN) SAM radar is locked on an American P-8A Poseidon making a Freedom of Navigation flight near Fiery Cross Reef. Two days later a second flight was intercepted by PLAAF J-11 Fighters. All subsequent USAF Recon/Freedom of Navigation flights were escorted by F-15Cs hastily deployed to the Philippines. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;December 2018; Additional tariffs and sanctions instituted on the PRC after the American Mid-term elections begin to bite. Unrest breaks out in several coastal cities as dock workers and other maritime trade workers suffer from work slowdowns. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;January 2019; frigid cold conditions across Asia caused great suffering in the DPRK and PRC. Dissatisfaction with the government spreads from the coastal areas inland where the suffering from the cold is greater. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;March 2019; Four PLAN aircraft from the carrier Liaoning intercept a pair of F-15J aircraft out of Naha AFB, Okinawa. During the intercept the Japanese pilots take evasive maneuvers leading to a dogfight like confrontation. While no missiles were launched several radar lock-on’s from both sides occurred. PRC and Japanese diplomats exchanged angry words in the U.N. Security Council. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;May 2019; Japanese Prime Minister Shinzo Abe announces plans to convert the Izumo Class DDHs to operate F-35Bs with a ski jump bow. The PRC immediately decries, "this move back toward militarism" and swore the Japanese move would not go unchallenged. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;June-July 2019; Nationalism in both Japan and the PRC rises to a fever pitch with million person marches in the streets of several Chinese cities. Late in July several Chinese fishing boats try to land protesters on the main island, Diaoyu Dao but are rebuffed by Japanese Coast Guard vessels.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;September 2019; a large fishing boat fleet carrying "peaceful protesters," ignoring typhoon warnings, sets out for the Diaoyu Islands. The typhoon, which was supposed to pass just east of the chain actually moves over the islands and five of the fishing boats with 199 passengers and crew are sunk. Despite Japanese rescue efforts, 99 Chinese drown, and Chinese media vows they will remember the "Sacred 99."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;October 2019; The Central Committee decides they need to distract attention from the failing economy and take advantage of the rising tide of nationalism. It is decided to take the Diaoyu Dao Islands right after the peak of typhoon season this November. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 14th: A PLAN amphibious exercise north of Taiwan continues with nothing seeming to be amiss. At sunset those vessels steam at flank speed for the Diaoyu Doa/Senkaku Islands. As they approach the island at dawn, escorted by two destroyers, the outgunned Japanese Coast Guard, "guard vessel" wisely withdraws. By noon, two reinforced companies of PRC Marines take control of the three main islands. Jubilation erupts across China with mass protests in Japan! The fuse has been lit. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 15th; "Black Friday" With Prime Minister Abe vowing the Chinese aggression will not stand, and PRC President Xi Jinping vowing the Diaoyu Dou will never be separated from China again, markets crash immediately after their open. The Shanghai Market loses 600 points almost 20 percent of its value. In America things are no better and by noon trading is halted on the Dow Jones with the greatest one day percentage loss in the index history. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 16th; The PRC reminds all parties of their self-declared Air Defense Intercept Zone (ADIZ) in the East China Sea and that that includes the Daioyu Dao Islands. Airlines quickly begin to cancel flights over the East China Sea and commercial vessels begin to flee the area and Chinese ports causing further economic disruption. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 18th; "Manic Monday" strikes the world markets! The Hang Seng and Shanghai Composite Indices both loose around 20 percent of their value. The contagion spreads to the European markets with the DAX and FTSE loosing 25 percent of their value but the worst was to come from Wall Street where the Dow Jones Industrials lost 9371.2 points a full 32 percent of its value and trading was finally halted at 1:35 p.m. Inside of four harrowing hours (with circuit breakers having kicked in) $11 trillion dollars of American wealth melted away! At 4:30 p.m. President Trump signed an emergency declaration closing trading for the remainder of the week and ordered to Federal Reserve to ship reserve currency to banks and other financial institutions to ensure there would be no run on the banks. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 19th; the recently formed 1,500 man strong Japanese Amphibious Rapid Deployment Brigade (ARDB) as seen loading onto LSTs in Kure Naval Base as the JS Kaga and escorts depart Sasebo Naval Base. USN Amphibious vessels also depart Sasebo and head for Okinawa to embark the USMC 31st Marine Expeditionary Unit (MEU). &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 20th; Both the DPRK and ROK declare neutrality in the current dispute. Both announce jointly that neither the PRC nor U.S. will be allowed to utilize Korean Bases. Privately assurances are relayed to Washington and Beijing that there will be no issues on the Korean Peninsula. The first USAF reinforcements, an F-35 Squadron from Elmendorf AFB, Alaska arrived at Kadena AFB with more in route.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 21st; Prime Minister Abe in a nationwide address, covered worldwide, announces that bases from the Home Islands would not be used in any effort to retake the islands but also announced that Japan would have its sovereignty restored either through diplomatic means or by force if necessary. Immediately following Abe’s address President Trump addressed the United States affirming the U.S. intention to meet its treaty obligations to Japan. Aircraft and SAM batteries from both the ROK and the Home Islands would be redeployed to the Ryukyu’s and especially to Okinawa. Further, additional bomber and support aircraft were to be dispatched to Anderson AFB, Guam. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 22nd; The PRC announces in the U.N. Security council they feel their East China Sea ADIZ to be legally binding and any aircraft entering it, including near the Daioyu Dao Islands risked being shot down. American RC-135 and P-8 recon aircraft continued to challenge the ADIZ with PLAAF interceptions but no hostile actions. The PRC also acknowledges the Japanese action and ensures if no home island bases are used they will be safe from attack, but if PRC forces are attacked any base utilized would be at risk of retaliation.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 23rd; The PRC and U.S. Ambassadors to the U.N. agree upon a meeting between the three leaders to occur in Singapore on Monday the 25th. While the world holds its collective breath both sides continue their build up. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 24th the Chinese carrier and her escorts run the Taiwan Straits into the East China Sea and is overflown by an American P-8 aircraft. American dependent evacuation from Okinawa and Japan begins. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 25th; at 05:32 a.m. Singapore time an American RQ-4 drone was shot down by an MPSAM over the Senkaku Islands. The world leader meeting at 09:00 quickly broke down into accusations and shouting with the PRC delegation walking out within an hour. Trading in the United States remains halted through the Thanksgiving holiday. President Trump vows to turn Okinawa into, "an unsinkable aircraft carrier!"&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 26-27th; relative quiet settles over the area as American and Japanese Recon aircraft remain close to the Ryukyu Islands but on the evening of the 27th an E-8 JSTARS aircraft is buzzed by a PLAAF J-20 with the approach so close the American aircraft had to dive injuring some crew. The American area commander announced that, "henceforth all recon aircraft will be escorted and any further risky intercepts could be met with deadly force."&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;November 28th, Thanksgiving Day in America, with world economies in shambles, major trade disrupted and the military confrontation growing hotter by the minute Americans sat around their tables praying for peace and for the troops along the Ryukyu’s front line. By evening a picket of PRC subs was west of the islands to block western SAGs/ARGs and with JMSDF and USN subs already deployed to the East China Sea. Two amphibious groups were at sea and ready for operations and the aircraft flooded the bases on both sides. All last minute hopes were dashed when at 6 p.m. EST the PRC Ambassador to the U.N. announced in the Security Council that, "as of 04:00 Daioyu Dao Islands time on Friday, 29 November ANY aircraft entering the East China Sea ADIZ WILL BE shot down without exception." Now with the world’s three largest economies about to go to war the world held its collective breath…&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Notes: &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;1. PRC units and western submarines teleport at one second into the game to allow for some randomness. Allow the game to run for a few seconds before giving any orders. There are three islands occupied on the island chain with on about 90 miles east of the other two. There are Amphibious Groups Supporting the ground forces ashore. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;2. I did set up ASW Missions around the ARGs, for the Western Subs and Okinawa CAP. Sorry they would be there and I don’t think reconfiguring or deleting three (3) missions is onerous to those who want to create your own. Players will want to just delete the Submarine Sea Control mission and reference points, it was established just to give the subs some motion after teleporting and until the players took over. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;3. For the most part, you hold the key to when hostiles begin, barring an unforeseen circumstance (pilots will be pilots) in the early stages. Read on…&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;4. The "Shelf Waters" or more shallow waters of the East China Sea (west of the trench west of the Ryukyu Chain) represents the Chinese ADIZ. Stay east of it until you are ready for war! &lt;I&gt;I have started you out with the PRC ADIZ Reference Points highlighted so once you know where the PRC will become hostile you can completely delete them thus avoiding needless clutter&lt;/I&gt;.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;5. If the PRC does not initiate hostilities, you should have about 6 to 8 hours prior to the optimum Western time to start of hostilities, so set up your missions and loadouts for the fighters and multirole aircraft. &lt;I&gt;Most aircraft with ready times less than 6 hours I put in reserve so you can choose your loadouts&lt;/I&gt;. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;6. Recon, MPA and Tankers with standard load outs are already set and aircraft ready times spread out because, well, no support unit readies every aircraft at once! &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;7. Bombers with long load out times I chose a load out I would use.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;8. Rules of Engagement, the JSDF/US player is not allowed to attack mainland PRC bases until your bases are attacked. This would constitute a major escalation in the war and I think most of us would agree most leaders would avoid that action unless they felt they had to. You have the resources to do it but National Command Authority will not give you a go until your bases are attacked themselves (realistically, I can’t stop you…) &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;9. Yes, the home island bases not being used is simply a convenient fiction, I needed to limit the number of units and single use airfields wouldn’t be right for this scenario as the PRC would attack them.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;10. The Japan-U.S. player’s greatest victory point opportunity (up to 10,000) comes from moving the amphibious groups to the islands and remaining there for at least 12 hours. You can receive, 2,000 pts for each of the helicopter carriers and 1,000 points each for the amphibious vessels and USS Blue Ridge. Good luck!&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;11. Don’t forget your forces on Guam!&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Created Under Version 1.14.5 - Build 998.12 &lt;FONT face="Calibri Light"&gt;–&lt;/FONT&gt; Data Base 745&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;An .INI and Delta File have been provided for rebuilds&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Al "BeirutDude" Sandrik (ex-Beachinnole)&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Al.Sandrik@gmail.com&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
